h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md:53:| 3, 4, 5b, 6a-prime | `exec agy` returned a short last message with no `<AUDIT_SENTINEL>` pair (`h_mad_extract_report.py` exit 2) | `<phase>:no_verdict` | "`agy --print` surfaces only the **last** message, so the report has one fragile channel (measured: 358 B of narration naming real Must-fix items, no sentinel — either a summarizing turn replaced the report or it was never emitted, i.e. **F-10 claim-execution divergence** per `AGENTS.md`; the remedy is the same either way). Do **not** apply the `exec` missing-report recovery — that is codex-scoped, and an audit leaves no tree delta. **But DO read `--log` first: it is no longer byte-identical to `--out`.** That equivalence held while agy ran in text mode and is now false — `exec agy` runs under `--output-format stream-json`, so the log holds the whole event stream: every tool call the reviewer made and every `agent_response` text_delta, including the ones from turns BEFORE the summarizing last message. That is precisely where a report lost to this failure mode still lives. Read it with `hmad-dispatch progress <log> --lines 50` (a digest; do not dump raw NDJSON), and if the findings are there, you have them without spending a cycle. **Then look for the FILE before concluding none was written (J30).** A run can honour neither transport and still write a real report, at a path of its own choosing: once as a workspace **dotfile** (`.design.audit.v14.md`, invisible to a `*audit.vN*` glob — which is how one cycle concluded 'no file was written' and re-dispatched over completed work), once into `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/` while narrating 'the current workspace'. `python3 h-mad/scripts/h_mad_offcontract_scan.py --cd "$CD" --expected "$RP"` searches both, dotfiles included, without assuming the `audit.vN` stem. A recovered file has had **no** schema enforcement applied to it — transcribe it into the gate's schema by hand and falsify every premise against the source (§"Verifying a review finding before acting on it"); never pipe it into the gate unread. Only when the stream shows the report was never emitted at all AND the scan finds nothing: re-read with `--from-start`, then `hmad-dispatch clear agy` and re-dispatch **with the report-file slot filled** + `report-wait "$RP"` (a file survives a later turn; last-message does not). **For 6a-prime, that wait needs `--no-done-marker` and the others must NOT have it.** `report-wait` treats `<path>.done` as the completion signal, and the archreview channel never asks for that marker: neither `agy-architectural-reviewer-prompt.md` nor the head contract `stage()` injects mentions it, which `h_mad_archreview_cycle._read_report_channel` records as the reason it deliberately does not require one either. So a bare `report-wait` on a 6a-prime report blocks for the whole `--timeout` (300s by default) and exits 1 on a COMPLETE, verdict-carrying file — measured: rc=1 after the full timeout, then rc=0 and the verdict returned immediately with the flag, on the same file. On an already-failed cycle that reads as "the re-dispatch wrote nothing" and costs another dispatch. It is opt-in per call and not a default precisely because phases 3/4/5b DO carry the marker in their contract (the codex implementer and verifier templates both create it, and `h_mad_audit_cycle._has_complete_report` requires it) — passing the flag there would turn off the one thing keeping a half-written audit report unreadable. Audits are idempotent — re-dispatch is safe. Never score the narration." |
